THE OPEN BUG, and how far it is narrowed
----------------------------------------

sprdma_and_dmc_dma disagrees with Mesen2 b9fa69d on exactly one row of sixteen:

    row      00  01  02  03  04 | 05 | 06  07  08  09  0A  0B  0C  0D  0E  0F
    Mesen   527 528 527 528 527  526  525 526 525 526 525 526 525 526 525 526
    ours    527 528 527 528 527  528  525 526 525 526 525 526 525 526 525 526

That is the FIRST ROM only. sprdma_and_dmc_dma_512 diverges on SIX rows - 04,
05, 06, 07, 0A and 0B - and Mesen emits 524 at row 04, a value this
implementation never produces anywhere. Both reference tables are in the header
of tests/test_files/fetch_dmc_dma.sh. The second ROM is the harder alignment
case and is where the remaining work is, not in row 05 of the first.

ALL MESEN POSITIONAL DATA IS DISCARDED. tools/mesen_event_dump.cpp samples
frames on a wall clock and loses rows (20s caught 12 of 16, 60s caught 13), so
every offset ever quoted from it is unsafe. Two sources are cycle-exact and
hole-free: this patch's tests/dmc_sweep_diag.cpp, and the ROM's own printed
table on both sides.

The bridge between them is established on OUR SIDE ALONE. The diagnostic reports
span 510 and no DMC inside for rows 0-5, span 512 and a collision for rows 6-15;
our ROM prints 527,528,527,528,527,528 then 525,526,... So 527/528 is the
UNCOLLIDED pair and 525/526 the COLLIDED one. Applying that to both tables:

    ours   ...527 528 | 525 526 ...      6 uncollided, 10 collided
    Mesen  ...527     | 526 525 ...      5 uncollided, 11 collided

OUR FIRST COLLISION IS ONE ITERATION LATE. Rows 06-0F are identical on both
sides; row 05 is uncollided for us, collided for Mesen.

At cycle resolution, in our own run, row 05 is:

    2064792  HALT  req1 load0            halt accepted
    2064793  dumy
    2064794  algn
    2064795  GET                         the read happens at 2064796
    2064797  idle req0 buf1 wrote1 oam1  sta $4014

The DMC DMA occupies 2064793-2064796 and the $4014 write lands at 2064797. IT
MISSES THE TRANSFER BY ONE CYCLE. The four cycles are individually correct - a
reload halts on a put, dummy on a get, one alignment cycle, read on a get - so
the fault is WHEN the sequence starts relative to the CPU reaching $4014, not
what it does once started.

First thing to explain: the three preceding DMC reads are 432 cycles apart
(2060881, 2061313, 2061745) and this one jumps 3050 to 2064796. That is neither
432 nor the 3424 of rate $00.

A LEAD FOUND AFTER PARKING, worth spending first
------------------------------------------------

The DMC start-delay parity table at apu.cpp:429 is currently REDUNDANT. The halt
cannot begin until the next get cycle (bus.cpp:318), so a 2-cycle delay lands on
the wrong phase and waits one while a 3-cycle delay does not - and both put the
halt on the SAME cycle. Measured by inverting the table to `? 3 : 2` and
rebuilding: every figure in tests/dmc_buffer_tests.cpp was unchanged and both
7-dmc_basics and 8-dmc_rates still passed.

So two mechanisms are performing one mechanism's alignment, and the phase gate
is the one that decides. Given that the symptom above is an entry that is ONE
CYCLE LATE, this is the first place to look. Found while widening the dmcBuffer
tests (2f029cd), not while working on this bug.

THE TWO NEGATIVE RESULTS, which are why this is parked
------------------------------------------------------

Both changes in (1) were built, measured, and kept only as evidence.

The first made the sweep monotonic - row 7 went -2 -> +2, span 510 -> 512, and
the table reached +10,+10 instead of stopping at +8,+8. The second removed the
last 2 cycles of entry delay at the crossing. Together the ten collision rows
now match Mesen exactly. The ROM still prints 05 528. Verified by stashing and
re-running, not assumed.

The reason was in the diagnostic's own output all along: THE `length` COLUMN
NEVER MOVED. 515,516,515,516... throughout. Only `span` and the offsets moved.
The ROM measures the LENGTH of the stall; the monotonicity argument measures
WHERE THE DMC LANDS INSIDE IT. Different quantities, and a change can fix one
while leaving the other untouched. Twice now.


IF YOU PICK THIS UP
-------------------

Explain the 3050-cycle gap first. Then decide between the only two branches
left: the DMC sequence started 2 cycles early, or the CPU reached $4014 2 cycles
late. Both are decidable inside our own cycle-stepped run. The apu.cpp:429 lead
above is the cheapest thing to eliminate before either.

DO NOT ADD ANOTHER FRAME-SAMPLED COMPARISON. The dump cost more than time: its
holes caused a TRUE finding to be discarded and a false refutation written down
(finding 11 "refuting" finding 9). If Mesen state is ever needed at cycle
resolution, drive it with the Step API instead of polling snapshots.

Standing of the findings in dmc_sweep_diag.cpp, after re-measuring frame-free:

    hold        1 (our half), 4, 7, 8, 12, 13
    conclusion  9 - true, but by finding 13's argument, not its own table
    no weight   2, 5, 10, 11 and 9's supporting table - all dump-derived

Finding 4 in particular records why a req/entry split CANNOT bisect this: the
ROM times itself with the DMC, so `req` moves when the DMA moves, and there is
no upstream in a closed loop.

Suite state. Master without this patch is 954 executed, 0 skipped, 2 failed -
the two SprdmaAndDmcDma rows above. Applying this adds the one diagnostic case,
so expect 955 / 0 / 2. NOT RE-MEASURED since the regeneration; the old header
said 955 / 0 / 5, and the three extra were the dmcBuffer tests, which were
over-strict rather than broken and have since been widened in 2f029cd. The same
failures occur with and without these changes. No regressions either way.

Sanitizers and scan-build were NOT run against these changes - they are not
candidates for commit, so analysing them would learn nothing.

