+//     there; the DMC simply does not move with respect to it.
+//
+//     Re-expressed against the DMA START instead of the first read, which is
+//     the boundary that actually decides a collision:
+//
+//       firstRead and dmcAbs are both always on get cycles, so `near` is always
+//       EVEN and cannot express a 1-cycle shift. `start` alternates parity with
+//       `pre`. Our crossing pair therefore sits at start-2 and start-1 - both
+//       BEFORE the DMA begins, so neither collides, which is what prints 528.
+//
+//     Mesen's second crossing row reaches the start cycle and collides. The
+//     remaining unknown is Mesen's own `pre` for that pair: near -2 means
+//     dmcAbs = start with pre 2, but start-1 with pre 3, and only the former
+//     is a collision. GET THAT NUMBER BEFORE THEORISING FURTHER - it decides
+//     whether our DMC read is 1-2 cycles early or our DMA start is late, and
+//     those are different bugs in different files.
+//
+//     tools/mesen_event_dump.cpp does not currently report the DMA start, only
+//     the sprite reads. Adding it is the next instrument change, and note that
+//     its 20s sweep returns 15 of 16 rows and says so - the missing row is at
+//     the END (Mesen's ROM table is 5 long + 11 short, so its sweep runs one
+//     further than ours), so the crossing pair IS present in the 15.
+//
+// 11. THE NUMBER FINDING 10 ASKED FOR, and it refutes finding 9's reading.
+//     tools/mesen_event_dump.cpp now reports `pre` too. Mesen, at the crossing:
+//
+//       span 510 cyc  pre 1  nearest DMC OAM  -4  inside 0
+//       span 510 cyc  pre 1  nearest DMC OAM  -2  inside 0
+//       span 512 cyc  pre 1  nearest DMC OAM  +2  inside 1
+//
+//     MESEN'S -2 ROW DOES NOT COLLIDE. inside 0, span 510. Finding 9 asserted
+//     that row was the collision printing 526; it is not, and the whole "our
+//     first collision is one iteration late" framing rests on that assumption.
+//     Mesen's crossing pair is -4,-2 with BOTH rows outside the transfer, and
+//     ours is -4,-4 with both outside. Neither side collides there.
+//
+//     So the 2-cycle difference at ROM row 05 is NOT a collision discount. What
+//     differs is only WHERE the non-colliding DMC sits: Mesen 2 cycles later
+//     than ours on the second row of the pair. Why that changes the ROM's
+//     measured length by 2 when the DMC is outside the transfer either way is
+//     now the question, and it is a different question from every one asked so
+//     far in this file.
+//
+//     Also measured: Mesen's `pre` alternates 1,2 where ours alternates 2,3, a
+//     constant +1. PROBABLY AN INSTRUMENT DIFFERENCE, NOT A BUG - our `start`
+//     is where ppu.dma_in_progress() first goes true, and the cycle trace shows
+//     that flag already set on the sta $4014 cycle (2213320, wrote1 oam1),
+//     while Mesen's first DmaRead event is the halt one cycle later. Confirm it
+//     before either trusting or acting on it; a constant offset is evidence
+//     about the instrument until proven otherwise.
+//
+// TARGET, as of finding 9: why the two OAM DMA alignments do not shift the DMC
+// read relative to the first sprite read the way Mesen's do. Compare the two
+// rows of the crossing pair against each other, not against the rest of the
+// sweep. Measure `length`, not offset or span - findings 7 and 8. The
+// state below predates that and is kept because it is still true, but note that
+// findings 2, 5 and 6 all reason about position and so do not bear on the ROM.
+//
+// Nothing here is cleared, because finding 4 cannot clear anything - see it.
+// What IS known:
+//
+//   * the collision discount (finding 1) is right, on both sides
+//   * the sweep must be monotonic and ours is not (findings 2, 5)
+//   * row 6 is the single anomalous row, and the single row where the entry
+//     phase gate fires
+//   * the gate cannot merely be deleted (finding 4)
+//
+// The open question is whether that gate's rule - a LOAD halts on a get, a
+// RELOAD on a put, wait otherwise - is the right way to keep the read on a get
+// cycle. It is enforced as a WAIT, which is what makes row 6 cost 2 extra
+// cycles; Mesen never waits, and reaches the same phase by choosing inside the
+// loop. Those differ only when the timer delivers a request at the "wrong"
+// phase, which happens on exactly one row of this sweep: row 6.
+//
+// 6. WHAT THE CYCLE TRACE SHOWS. Row 6's two extra cycles are accounted for
+//    exactly, and neither is a bug on its own (state shown is what
+//    advance_dmc_dma set at the END of that cycle, so it applies on the next):
+//
+//      2213320 par0 idle req1 load0 wrote1 oam1   sta $4014 - halt refused,
+//                                                 the CPU is writing
+//      2213321 par1 idle req1 load0 wrote0 oam1   phase gate refuses:
+//                                                 next_is_get=1 != is_load=0
+//      2213322 par0 algn req1 load0 wrote0 oam1   accepted
+//      2213323 par1 GET
+//
+//    One cycle of write refusal, one of parity. Every clean row shows the
+//    undisturbed HALT/dumy/algn/GET with no wait at all, so the gate normally
+//    costs nothing and only bites when a request lands beside the $4014 write.
+//
+//    THE MECHANISM THIS SUGGESTS - derived from Mesen's loop, NOT yet measured,
+//    so treat it as the next experiment and not as a finding. During an OAM DMA
+//    Mesen absorbs the DMC's halt and dummy into two sprite cycles
+//    (NesCpu.cpp:384 processCycle, and the comment at :385 "Sprite DMA cycles
+//    count as halt/dummy cycles for the DMC DMA when both run at the same
+//    time"), then reads at the next get cycle. We instead go straight to Align
+//    and read one cycle later. Both extend the OAM DMA by 2 - which is why
+//    finding 1 measures the discount as correct on both sides - but the LATENCY
+//    from request to read differs. Writing C for the cycle the request is first
+//    accepted-or-refused:
+//
+//      C even   ours reads at C+2   Mesen reads at C+4    <- differs by 2
+//      C odd    ours reads at C+3   Mesen reads at C+3       agrees
+//
+//    A difference that appears on one parity only is the right shape for a
+//    defect that fires on exactly one row of a sweep that steps the DMC past
+//    the sprite DMA two cycles at a time.
+//
+//    To test it, the collision path has to spend two cycles being halted before
+//    the read WITHOUT stealing them from the OAM DMA - the halt and dummy are
+//    free precisely because the CPU is already stopped. That is a restructure
+//    of the Align branch, not a parity tweak, and it must keep the read on a get
+//    cycle (see finding 4) and keep the span at 512 (finding 1).
+//
+// 7. THAT RESTRUCTURE WAS BUILT AND MEASURED, and it changes the ROM's verdict
+//    by NOTHING. CollisionHalt/CollisionDummy in Bus::advance_dmc_dma made this
+//    sweep monotonic - row 7 went -2 -> +2, its span 510 -> 512, and the table
+//    reaches +10,+10 where it used to stop at +8,+8, matching Mesen's endpoint
+//    and its ten collision rows. The ROM still prints 05 528 against Mesen's
+//    05 526, byte for byte the same sixteen numbers as without it. Verified by
+//    stashing the change and re-running, not assumed.
+//
+//    WHY, and this invalidates the target in finding 6 and most of this file's
+//    reasoning: THE `length` COLUMN NEVER MOVED. It reads 515,516,515,516...
+//    with and without the restructure. Only `span` and the offsets moved. The
+//    ROM measures the LENGTH of the stall; this file's monotonicity argument
+//    measures WHERE the DMC lands inside it. Those are different quantities,
+//    and a change can fix one while leaving the other untouched - which is
+//    precisely what happened.
+//
+//    So "the sweep must be monotonic" was never a route to row 05. It is a real
+//    property and ours really did violate it, but it is not the ROM's verdict
+//    and fixing it does not move the ROM. Three of this file's five findings
+//    were reasoning about position while the oracle was reporting duration.
+//
+// 8. THE ACTUAL SHAPE OF THE DEFECT, from the two tables side by side:
+//
+//      Mesen  527 528 527 528 527 | 526 525 526 525 526 525 526 525 526 525 526
+//      ours   527 528 527 528 527   528 | 525 526 525 526 525 526 525 526 525 526
+//
+//    Both are a run of LONG rows followed by a run of SHORT ones. Mesen crosses
+//    after 5 long rows; we cross after 6. Our first collision happens one
+//    iteration LATE, and row 05 is long for us and short for Mesen because of
+//    it. Everything after the crossing already agrees, which is why 15 of 16
+//    rows match and always have.
+//
+//    That is a question about WHEN the DMC first collides - i.e. about the DMC
+//    timer's phase relative to the ROM's $4014 write, one iteration before the
+//    crossing - and NOT about what a collision costs once it happens, which
+//    finding 1 measured correct on both sides and which finding 7 has now shown
+//    is insensitive to the whole Align/Collision restructure.
+#include <gtest/gtest.h>
+
+#include <cstdio>
+#include <string>
+#include <vector>
+
+#include "bus.h"
+
+namespace
+{
+// Enough sprite DMAs to see the crossing; Mesen showed it after three.
+constexpr size_t kWantedSamples = 24;
+
+// A ceiling, not a budget. Mesen saw the first sprite DMA around 48 frames in;
+// this is far above that so a ROM that gets further does not silently truncate.
+constexpr uint64_t kMaxMasterTicks = 400ULL * 341 * 262 * 4;
+
+struct Sample {
+    uint64_t length = 0;  // CPU cycles the OAM DMA held the bus
+    long dmcOffset = -1;  // CPU cycles from the FIRST SPRITE READ to the DMC get
+    bool sawDmc = false;
+
+    // First and last sprite read, so the span is measured exactly as Mesen's is.
+    //
+    // Measuring from DMA START instead confounds the comparison: the gap from
+    // start to first read is the halt plus an OPTIONAL alignment cycle, so it is
+    // itself parity-dependent - and our lengths alternate 515/516, so that gap
+    // alternates too. An offset measured from the start then steps by 1 even if
+    // the DMC is moving by 2. That artifact has exactly the shape of the bug
+    // being hunted, so it cannot be left in the instrument.
+    uint64_t firstRead = 0;
+    uint64_t lastRead = 0;
+    bool sawRead = false;
+    uint64_t dmcAbs = 0;  // absolute cycle of the DMC get, resolved at DMA end
+
+    // The two points UPSTREAM of the get, which bisect the bug:
+    //
+    //   request  APU::dmc.transfer_requested goes true - the DMC timer's doing,
+    //            nothing to do with the DMA state machine
+    //   entry    advance_dmc_dma leaves Idle - the halt is finally accepted,
+    //            after the parity gate and the write-cycle refusal
+    //
+    // The ROM walks the sprite DMA past the DMC one cycle per row, so BOTH must
+    // move monotonically across the sweep. Whichever one jumps is the culprit:
+    // a non-monotonic request means the DMC timer is being perturbed upstream,
+    // and a monotonic request with a non-monotonic entry means the fault is in
+    // advance_dmc_dma's entry conditions.
+    // Nearest DMC read to the first sprite read, recorded on EVERY row
+    // including the ones where the DMC misses the transfer. Finding 8.
+    // Absolute cycle the OAM DMA became active, so the pre-read overhead
+    // (firstRead - start) can be compared between the two alignments of a
+    // pair. Finding 9 asks what differs between them; this is the difference.
+    uint64_t start = 0;
+    bool haveNear = false;
+    long nearOffset = 0;
+    long reqOffset = 0;
+    long entryOffset = 0;
+    bool haveReq = false;
+    bool haveEntry = false;
+};
+
+// Nearest recorded cycle to `to`, so a request that fired before the DMA began
+// is still attributed to it. Returns false when nothing was recorded at all.
+bool nearest_to(const std::vector<uint64_t>& cycles, uint64_t to, long& out)
+{
+    bool found = false;
+    long best = 0;
+    for (const uint64_t c : cycles) {
+        const long d = static_cast<long>(c) - static_cast<long>(to);
+        if (!found || std::labs(d) < std::labs(best)) {
+            best = d;
+            found = true;
+        }
+    }
+    out = best;
+    return found;
+}
+}  // namespace
+
+GTEST_TEST(dmcSweepDiag, sprite_dma_length_and_dmc_offset)
+{
+    const std::string path = std::string(NES_TEST_FILES_DIR) + "/dmc_dma/sprdma_and_dmc_dma.nes";
+
+    Bus console;
+    ASSERT_TRUE(console.load_cartridge(path))
+        << "could not load " << path << " - run tests/test_files/fetch_dmc_dma.sh";
+    console.cpu.reset();  // without this the CPU never loads its reset vector and
+                          // the whole run is 143M ticks of nothing happening
+
+    std::vector<Sample> samples;
+    bool previouslyInDma = false;
+    uint64_t dmaStart = 0;
+    uint64_t lastDmcCycle = UINT64_MAX;
+    uint16_t previousSrcAddr = 0;
+    bool previousWanted = false;
+    bool previousDmcActive = false;
+    uint64_t lastTraceCycle = UINT64_MAX;
+    // EVERY DMC read, not just the ones landing inside an OAM DMA. `dmcAbs`
+    // below is only recorded during a DMA, so the six rows where the DMC misses
+    // the sprite transfer entirely print "no DMC inside" and nothing else - and
+    // those are precisely the rows that decide WHERE THE CROSSING IS, which is
+    // the open question (finding 8). Without this the instrument cannot see the
+    // thing being investigated.
+    std::vector<uint64_t> dmcGets;
+    std::vector<uint64_t> requests;
+    std::vector<uint64_t> entries;
+    Sample current;
+
+    for (uint64_t tick = 0; tick < kMaxMasterTicks && samples.size() < kWantedSamples; ++tick) {
+        console.clock();
+
+        // Bus::clock performs the DMC read on the CPU tick that ENTERS the Get
+        // state (bus.cpp:259) and advance_dmc_dma then returns it to Idle, so
+        // from out here Get is observed on the cycle BEFORE the read. That is a
+        // constant, and constants do not affect step or delta - see the header.
+        // Deduplicated on cpu_cycles because one CPU cycle spans several master
+        // ticks and would otherwise be counted repeatedly.
+        if (console.dmc_dma == Bus::DmcDma::Get && console.cpu_cycles != lastDmcCycle) {
+            lastDmcCycle = console.cpu_cycles;
+            dmcGets.push_back(console.cpu_cycles + 1);  // same +1 as below
+            if (previouslyInDma) {
+                // +1: the READ happens on the cycle AFTER this observation.
+                // Bus::clock performs it when dmc_dma is already Get on entry
+                // (bus.cpp:259) and advance_dmc_dma returns it to Idle before
+                // clock() returns, so Get is only ever visible from out here on
+                // the preceding cycle.
+                //
+                // Without this the offsets come out ODD where Mesen's are EVEN,
+                // which reads as our DMC get landing on the opposite phase to
+                // the sprite reads - a wrong-phase bug that does not exist, and
+                // a fix to the parity gate that would have broken working code.
+                current.dmcAbs = console.cpu_cycles + 1;
+                current.sawDmc = true;
+            }
+        }
+
+        // Rising edges only, so a level that stays true for several cycles is
+        // recorded once - at the cycle it became true, which is the thing the
+        // sweep moves.
+        const bool wanted = console.apu.dmc_wants_sample_byte();
+        if (wanted && !previousWanted) {
+            requests.push_back(console.cpu_cycles);
+        }
+        previousWanted = wanted;
+
+        const bool dmcActive = console.dmc_dma != Bus::DmcDma::Idle;
+        if (dmcActive && !previousDmcActive) {
+            entries.push_back(console.cpu_cycles);
+        }
+        previousDmcActive = dmcActive;
+
+        // A sprite read is detected by the source address ADVANCING, which is
+        // what Mesen's page-marching reads are too. Watching remaining_dma_cycles
+        // instead would count halt and alignment cycles as reads.
+        const bool inDma = console.ppu.dma_in_progress();
+        if (inDma) {
+            const uint16_t src = console.ppu.dma_current_memory_source_addr;
+            if (src != previousSrcAddr) {
+                if (!current.sawRead) {
+                    current.firstRead = console.cpu_cycles;
+                    current.sawRead = true;
+                }
+                current.lastRead = console.cpu_cycles;
+                previousSrcAddr = src;
+            }
+        }
+
+        if (inDma && !previouslyInDma) {
+            dmaStart = console.cpu_cycles;
+            current = Sample{};
+            current.start = dmaStart;
+            previousSrcAddr = console.ppu.dma_current_memory_source_addr;
+        } else if (!inDma && previouslyInDma) {
+            current.length = console.cpu_cycles - dmaStart;
+            if (current.sawDmc && current.sawRead) {
+                current.dmcOffset = static_cast<long>(current.dmcAbs) - static_cast<long>(current.firstRead);
+            }
+            if (current.sawRead) {
+                current.haveNear = nearest_to(dmcGets, current.firstRead, current.nearOffset);
+                current.haveReq = nearest_to(requests, current.firstRead, current.reqOffset);
+                current.haveEntry = nearest_to(entries, current.firstRead, current.entryOffset);
+            }
+            samples.push_back(current);
+        }
+
+        // CYCLE TRACE across rows 6-7, the anomalous pair. Rows 0-5 are clean
+        // and row 8 onwards is clean-but-offset, so the whole defect is in this
+        // window. Enabled once six samples exist, which is after row 5's DMA
+        // ended and BEFORE row 6's request - the request is raised while the CPU
+        // is still executing, so a window starting at row 6's DMA would miss the
+        // very thing being measured.
+        //
+        // Printed only while a request is outstanding or the DMA is running,
+        // which is ~20 lines per row rather than the ~180,000 cycles between
+        // them. `wrote` is Bus::cpu_wrote_this_cycle, which is the halt refusal;
+        // `par` is the get/put phase advance_dmc_dma gates on.
+        if (samples.size() == 4 || samples.size() == 5) {
+            const bool interesting = wanted || dmcActive || (inDma && !current.sawRead);
+            if (interesting && console.cpu_cycles != lastTraceCycle) {
+                lastTraceCycle = console.cpu_cycles;
+                static const char* kState[] = {"idle", "HALT", "dumy", "algn", "GET ", "cHLT", "cDUM"};
+                std::printf("    trace %6llu par%d %s req%d load%d buf%d wrote%d oam%d%s\n",
+                            static_cast<unsigned long long>(console.cpu_cycles),
+                            static_cast<int>(console.cpu_cycles % 2), kState[static_cast<int>(console.dmc_dma)], wanted,
+                            console.apu.dmc_transfer_is_load(), console.apu.dmc_sample_buffer_filled(),
+                            console.cpu_wrote_this_cycle, inDma, (inDma && !current.sawRead) ? "  <- pre-read" : "");
+            }
+        }
+
+        previouslyInDma = inDma;
+    }
+
+    std::printf("\nours - one line per sprite DMA:\n");
+    long previousOffset = 0;
+    bool havePrevious = false;
+    uint64_t previousFirstRead = 0;
+    bool havePreviousRead = false;
+    for (const Sample& s : samples) {
+        // Cycles between consecutive sprite DMAs. KEPT ONLY AS A NEGATIVE
+        // RESULT - it does not discriminate anything, and the reasoning that
+        // added it was wrong.
+        //
+        // The idea was that the ROM's loop must shorten by exactly the change in
+        // stall cost, so ITER would fall by 2 at the crossing and a rise would
+        // convict. That assumed a tight loop. It is not one: iterations run
+        // ~178,000 cycles apart and vary by ~30,000 between neighbours, because
+        // they are dominated by vblank waits and by printing the previous row.
+        // A 2-cycle effect is far below that noise floor and always will be.
+        //
+        // Left in place so the next reader does not re-derive the same idea and
+        // re-measure it. If a per-iteration cycle budget is ever needed, it has
+        // to come from the ROM's own timer code, not from wall-to-wall spacing.
+        if (havePreviousRead) {
+            std::printf("  iter %5lld", static_cast<long long>(s.firstRead - previousFirstRead));
+        } else {
+            std::printf("  iter     -");
+        }
+        previousFirstRead = s.firstRead;
+        havePreviousRead = s.sawRead;
+
+        std::printf("  length %4llu  span %4lld  ", static_cast<unsigned long long>(s.length),
+                    static_cast<long long>(s.lastRead - s.firstRead));
+        if (s.sawDmc) {
+            std::printf("DMC at OAM%+5ld", s.dmcOffset);
+            if (havePrevious) {
+                std::printf("  step %+ld", s.dmcOffset - previousOffset);
+            }
+            previousOffset = s.dmcOffset;
+            havePrevious = true;
+        } else {
+            std::printf("no DMC inside       ");
+        }
+        if (s.haveReq) {
+            std::printf("  pre%2lld", static_cast<long long>(s.firstRead - s.start));
+            std::printf("  near%+5ld", s.haveNear ? s.nearOffset : 0);
+            std::printf("  req%+5ld", s.reqOffset);
+        }
+        if (s.haveEntry) {
+            std::printf("  entry%+5ld", s.entryOffset);
+        }
+        std::printf("\n");
+    }
+    std::printf("(%zu sprite DMAs observed)\n", samples.size());
+
+    // The only real assertion: that the diagnostic measured anything at all. A
+    // table of zero rows would otherwise read as "our emulator performs no
+    // sprite DMA", which is a claim this file is not entitled to make.
+    ASSERT_FALSE(samples.empty()) << "no sprite DMA observed in " << kMaxMasterTicks << " master ticks";
+}
